This empty-dependency `useEffect` runs non-idempotent initialization on mount: `enablePolicyTaxes(policyID, false)` mutates Onyx data, and `onConnect(false)` triggers navigation / opens an external setup link. React Strict Mode intentionally double-invokes mount effects in development, so this logic will run twice — firing a duplicate policy write and a duplicate navigation/open-link. The `eslint-disable react-hooks/exhaustive-deps` suppresses the deps warning but there is no guard against double execution.

Guard the one-time init with a ref (or module-level flag) so it runs exactly once:

```tsx
const didInit = useRef(false);

useEffect(() => {
    if (didInit.current) {
        return;
    }
    didInit.current = true;

    enablePolicyTaxes(policyID, false);
    if (environment === CONST.ENVIRONMENT.DEV && !isAuthError) {
        setIsConnectionOptionsPopoverOpen(true);
        return;
    }
    onConnect(false);
    // eslint-disable-next-line react-hooks/exhaustive-deps
}, []);
```

This `useEffect` runs with an empty dependency array `[]` and its only work is a `Navigation.navigate(...)` call. Navigation is explicitly non-idempotent — under React Strict Mode this effect is intentionally double-invoked in development, which can fire the navigation twice and push a duplicate route. The `eslint-disable`/"run once" comment only silences the exhaustive-deps lint; it is not an execution guard.

Add a real one-time guard so the navigation runs exactly once:

```tsx
let didInit = false;

function ConnectToDualEntryFlow({policyID}: ConnectToDualEntryFlowProps) {
    const hasReusablePoliciesConnectedToDualEntry = useHasReusablePoliciesConnectedTo(CONST.POLICY.CONNECTIONS.NAME.DUALENTRY, policyID);

    useEffect(() => {
        if (didInit) {
            return;
        }
        didInit = true;

        if (hasReusablePoliciesConnectedToDualEntry) {
            Navigation.navigate(ROUTES.POLICY_ACCOUNTING_DUALENTRY_EXISTING_CONNECTIONS.getRoute(policyID));
            return;
        }
        Navigation.navigate(ROUTES.POLICY_ACCOUNTING_DUALENTRY_SETUP.getRoute(policyID));
        // eslint-disable-next-line react-hooks/exhaustive-deps
    }, []);

    return null;
}
```

This effect runs once on mount (`[]` dependency array) and performs non-idempotent work: it dismisses the modal and then triggers a redirect via `startDistanceRequest`/`startMoneyRequest`/`Navigation.navigate`. React Strict Mode intentionally double-invokes effects in development, so these navigation/request-start calls can fire twice, potentially opening two flows or starting duplicate money requests. The existing `if (shouldShowNotFound) return;` is a precondition check, not an execution guard — it does not prevent the redirect logic from running again on a second invocation.

Add a module-level (or ref-based) guard that both checks and sets a flag before performing the redirect, e.g.:

```tsx
let didRedirect = false;

// inside the effect
if (shouldShowNotFound) {
    return;
}
if (didRedirect) {
    return;
}
didRedirect = true;
Navigation.dismissModal();
// ...redirect logic
```

This `useEffect` has an empty dependency array and performs `Navigation.navigate(...)`, which is non-idempotent. React Strict Mode intentionally double-invokes effects in development, so this effect can fire two navigations on mount. There is no guard (module-level flag or ref) preventing the second run — the `if (hasReusablePoliciesConnectedToRillet)` check validates a precondition but does not stop re-execution if the same precondition still holds on the second invocation.

Guard the one-time initialization so it runs exactly once regardless of rendering mode:

```tsx
const didInit = useRef(false);

useEffect(() => {
    if (didInit.current) {
        return;
    }
    didInit.current = true;

    if (hasReusablePoliciesConnectedToRillet) {
        Navigation.navigate(ROUTES.POLICY_ACCOUNTING_RILLET_EXISTING_CONNECTIONS.getRoute(policyID));
        return;
    }
    Navigation.navigate(ROUTES.POLICY_ACCOUNTING_RILLET_SETUP.getRoute(policyID));
    // eslint-disable-next-line react-hooks/exhaustive-deps
}, []);
```

This `useEffect` has an empty dependency array `[]`, so React Strict Mode intentionally double-invokes it in development. The newly added `clearMoneyRequest(...)` is non-idempotent — it deletes draft transactions (`getRemoveDraftTransactionsByIDsData` sets the draft keys to `null` via `Onyx.multiSet`). The existing `if (!isIouTypeValid || !isIouRequestTypeValid) return;` is only a precondition check, not an execution guard, so the deletion (and the navigation that follows) can run twice. A second invocation could clear a draft the destination start page has already begun rebuilding, reintroducing the stale-shape race this PR is trying to fix.

Add a module-level guard that both checks and sets a flag so the init logic runs exactly once, e.g.:

```tsx
let didInit = false;

function IOURequestRedirectToStartPage({route}: IOURequestRedirectToStartPageProps) {
    // ...
    useEffect(() => {
        if (!isIouTypeValid || !isIouRequestTypeValid) {
            return;
        }
        if (didInit) {
            return;
        }
        didInit = true;

        clearMoneyRequest(CONST.IOU.OPTIMISTIC_TRANSACTION_ID, draftTransactionIDs ?? [CONST.IOU.OPTIMISTIC_TRANSACTION_ID]);
        // ...
    }, []);
}
```

This `useEffect` fires `seedMyExpensesSearch`, which is non-idempotent — it dispatches `API.write(WRITE_COMMANDS.SAVE_SEARCH, ...)`. The only guard is `hasSeededMyExpensesSearch`, an Onyx NVP that is set *optimistically* and asynchronously. Because the optimistic `Onyx.METHOD.SET` is batched, the effect can re-run (e.g. on a dependency change such as `allPolicies` updating, or under React Strict Mode's double-invoke) before the NVP propagates back, producing duplicate `SAVE_SEARCH` calls. A data/precondition check is explicitly called out by the rule as NOT a real execution guard.

Add a synchronous execution guard (module-level or ref) that is checked and set in the same tick, in addition to the NVP gate:

```tsx
const hasSeededRef = useRef(false);

useEffect(() => {
    if (hasSeededRef.current || hasSeededMyExpensesSearch || currentUserAccountID === -1 || !currentUserEmail || allPolicies === undefined) {
        return;
    }
    if (isDualRoleUser(allPolicies, currentUserEmail)) {
        hasSeededRef.current = true;
        seedMyExpensesSearch(currentUserAccountID, translate('search.mySavedSearch'));
    }
}, [hasSeededMyExpensesSearch, currentUserAccountID, currentUserEmail, allPolicies, translate]);
```

This `useEffect` has an empty dependency array and runs non-idempotent initialization: `enablePolicyTaxes(policyID, false)` dispatches an optimistic Onyx merge and an API write. React Strict Mode intentionally double-invokes mount effects in development, so this would fire the tax-disable request twice. The `eslint-disable react-hooks/exhaustive-deps` and the existing comment validate the precondition but do not guard against re-execution.

Add a module-level guard so the mutation runs exactly once:

```tsx
let didDisableTaxes = false;

function QuickbooksOnlineSetupPage({route}: QuickbooksOnlineSetupPageProps) {
    // ...
    useEffect(() => {
        if (didDisableTaxes) {
            return;
        }
        didDisableTaxes = true;
        // Since QBO doesn't support Taxes, we should disable them from the LHN when connecting to QBO
        enablePolicyTaxes(policyID, false);
        // eslint-disable-next-line react-hooks/exhaustive-deps
    }, []);
}
```

`clearNewRoomFormError()` is called inside a `useEffect` with an empty dependency array `[]` and no execution guard. This function performs a mutation (clearing form errors in Onyx), which is not idempotent. In React Strict Mode, this effect will run twice, causing a double clear.

Add a module-level guard to prevent double execution:
```tsx
let didInit = false;

function WorkspaceRoomCreatePage({route}: WorkspaceRoomCreatePageProps) {
    // ...
    useEffect(() => {
        if (didInit) {
            return;
        }
        didInit = true;
        clearNewRoomFormError();
    }, []);
    // ...
}
```

`Navigation.navigate()` is not idempotent -- calling it twice produces undesirable results. The `consumeNavigationToken()` check is a precondition check, not a double-execution guard. In React Strict Mode, this effect runs twice: the first invocation consumes the token and returns early, but the second invocation finds the token already consumed and triggers an unwanted navigation to `SETTINGS_AGENTS_ADD`.

Add a module-level or ref-based guard to prevent double execution:

```tsx
const didInitRef = useRef(false);

useEffect(() => {
    if (didInitRef.current) {
        return;
    }
    didInitRef.current = true;

    if (consumeNavigationToken()) {
        return;
    }
    Navigation.navigate(ROUTES.SETTINGS_AGENTS_ADD);
}, []);
```

`initReconnect()` registers event listeners via `subscribeNetworkState` and `addBecameActiveListener`, which are not idempotent -- calling them twice would create duplicate subscriptions. In React Strict Mode, this `useEffect` with an empty dependency array will execute twice, resulting in duplicate listener registrations.

Add a module-level guard to prevent double execution:

```tsx
let didInit = false;

// ...

useEffect(() => {
    if (didInit) {
        return;
    }
    didInit = true;
    initReconnect();
}, []);
```

Alternatively, add the guard inside `initReconnect()` itself so all call sites are protected.

The ref-based guard does not fully prevent double execution. The ref `hasLoadedCardSettingsRef` is only set to `true` when `openPolicyExpensifyCardsPage` is actually called (line 143). In the most common scenario described in the PR -- where the parent already loaded the data (`hasOnceLoaded` is `true`) -- the effect returns early and the ref stays `false`. When the backend `SET` response later wipes `hasOnceLoaded`, the ref is still `false`, so the guard falls through and the unnecessary API call fires anyway. The fix only prevents a *second* unnecessary reload, not the *first* one.

Restructure the effect so the ref is set to `true` whenever data is already loaded, not just when the API call is made:

```tsx
useEffect(() => {
    if (hasLoadedCardSettingsRef.current || !expensifyCardSettings || expensifyCardSettings?.isLoading) {
        return;
    }
    hasLoadedCardSettingsRef.current = true;
    if (expensifyCardSettings?.hasOnceLoaded) {
        return;
    }
    openPolicyExpensifyCardsPage(policyID, defaultFundID);
}, [defaultFundID, expensifyCardSettings, policyID]);
```

This way, once the effect observes that data has been loaded (either by the parent or by this effect), the ref prevents any future invocations from triggering the API call even if `hasOnceLoaded` gets wiped by a backend `SET` response.

`NetworkState.initAppForegroundListener()` is called inside a `useEffect` without a guard against double-execution. This function calls `AppStateMonitor.addBecameActiveListener(...)` which registers a new listener each time. In React Strict Mode (development), the effect fires twice, registering the listener twice and causing duplicate `reconnect()` calls on every app foreground event.

Either add a module-level guard inside `initAppForegroundListener` to prevent double-registration:
```typescript
let listenerRegistered = false;
function initAppForegroundListener() {
    if (listenerRegistered) {
        return;
    }
    listenerRegistered = true;
    AppStateMonitor.addBecameActiveListener(() => {
        // ...
    });
}
```
Or return a cleanup function from `initAppForegroundListener` and use it in the effect's cleanup.

