This mount-only `useEffect` performs non-idempotent initialization (`NetworkConnection.listenForReconnect()`, `PusherConnectionManager.init()`, `initializePusher()`, `App.openApp()`/`App.reconnectApp()`, `Download.clearDownloads()`, etc.) without a guard against double execution.

Critically, this component is rendered in **both** the delegator loading branch (inside a `<>` Fragment) and the main return branch (inside `<ComposeProviders>`) of `AuthScreens`. When the delegator flow completes and `isDelegatorFromOldDotIsReady` flips to `true`, React unmounts the loading tree and mounts the main tree because the parent elements differ. This causes `AuthScreensInitHandler` to unmount (running `Session.cleanupSession()`) and then remount (re-running the entire initialization effect a second time). The original code was protected from this because the loading branch only rendered `<FullScreenLoadingIndicator />` without any handler components.

Consider adding a module-level guard to prevent double initialization:
```tsx
let didInit = false;

// Inside the mount-only useEffect:
useEffect(() => {
    if (didInit) {
        return;
    }
    didInit = true;
    // ... rest of initialization logic ...

    return () => {
        didInit = false;
        Session.cleanupSession();
    };
}, []);
```

